# Derivative-Free Optimization 4: Black Boxes

A black box is a function you can call but not look inside: a simulation, a solver run, a measurement. It may fail on some inputs, return several objectives, report constraints alongside, and cost seconds per call. This chapter covers the `blackbox` interface and the mesh adaptive solver behind it, `MadsAdvanced`. After it you can report failures honestly, use the cache, trade several objectives with weights, and choose how constraints are treated.

Every program is complete and its output is the output of that program.

## The interface

Instead of `objective`, set `blackbox`. It returns a `BlackboxEvaluation`:

| Field | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `objectives` | one or more real values (a vector) | required |
| `constraints` | zero or more values, feasible when every entry is `<= 0` | none |
| `evaluation_ok` | `false` marks the call as a failure; its values are ignored | `true` |
| `count_evaluation` | `false` keeps the call out of the evaluation budget (use it for cheap pre-checks) | `true` |

Setting `blackbox` makes `Auto` choose `MadsAdvanced`. You can also pass a blackbox to `OrthoMads` or `LtMads`, which share its machinery.

The options live in `DfoOptions::mads`:

| Option | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `constraint_handling` | `Ignore`, `ExtremeBarrier`, `ProgressiveBarrier` | `Ignore` |
| `progressive_barrier_penalty` | merit penalty per unit of violation | 100 |
| `multiobjective_scalarization` | `WeightedSum` or `Chebyshev` | `WeightedSum` |
| `objective_weights`, `objective_reference` | weights, and the reference point for `Chebyshev` | equal weights |
| `enable_cache`, `cache_round_digits` | remember evaluated points (rounded to this many digits) | on, 12 |
| `search_points` | extra trial points per iteration before the poll | 0 |
| `surrogate_mode` | `Off` or `CandidateOrdering` (rank candidates with a cheap local model) | `Off` |

